::PHY-U1-L6-Q01::A system in physics is {=an object or group of objects selected for analysis ~every object in the universe ~only a single moving object ~only the forces outside a boundary} #### A system is the chosen object or group being analyzed.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q02::A system boundary separates {=the system from its environment ~mass from weight ~speed from velocity ~action from reaction} #### The boundary defines what is inside and outside the system.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q03::For two blocks treated as one system, the tension between them is {=internal ~external ~gravitational only ~zero in every case} #### Tension between objects inside the boundary is internal.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q04::For one block alone, a rope tension exerted by an outside rope is {=external ~internal ~not a force ~always balanced} #### The rope is outside the chosen one-block system.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q05::Internal force pairs cancel in the net-force equation for the whole system because they are {=equal in magnitude and opposite in direction ~both zero ~both friction forces ~perpendicular} #### Newton's third-law internal pairs sum to zero for the whole system.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q06::Which force changes the momentum of an isolated system? {=A net external force ~An internal force pair ~The system boundary ~The center of mass alone} #### System momentum changes because of net external force.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q07::Two blocks of total mass 10 kg experience a net external force of 30 N. Their acceleration is {=3 m/s^2 ~0.33 m/s^2 ~30 m/s^2 ~300 m/s^2} #### Use a=Fnet/m=30/10.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q08::When two connected blocks accelerate together, they have {=the same acceleration magnitude ~the same mass ~zero tension ~opposite velocities} #### A taut connection constrains their accelerations.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q09::An Atwood machine normally consists of {=two masses connected by a string over a pulley ~a block on a spring ~a rotating disk only ~a projectile and target} #### This is the standard Atwood-machine model.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q10::In an ideal Atwood machine with unequal masses, the heavier mass accelerates {=downward ~upward ~horizontally ~not at all} #### The weight difference produces the net driving force.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q11::For an ideal Atwood system, the driving external force magnitude is {=the difference between the two weights ~the sum of the tensions ~zero ~the normal force} #### Subtract the opposing weights for the two-mass system.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q12::If equal masses hang on an ideal Atwood machine, its acceleration is {=zero ~g ~2g ~dependent only on string length} #### Equal weights balance.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q13::A free-body diagram should include {=forces acting on the selected object only ~forces the object exerts on everything else ~velocity arrows as forces ~internal details outside the boundary} #### Draw only forces acting on the selected object.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q14::A normal force is exerted {=perpendicular to a contact surface ~parallel to every surface ~only by gravity ~toward the center of Earth} #### Normal means perpendicular to the surface.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q15::Static friction adjusts up to {=a maximum value ~exactly zero ~the object's weight in every case ~twice the kinetic friction in every case} #### Static friction matches the applied force until its maximum is reached.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q16::Once an object slides, the relevant friction model is usually {=kinetic friction ~static friction only ~buoyant force ~spring force} #### Sliding surfaces experience kinetic friction.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q17::An object has 12 N right and 7 N left. Net force is {=5 N right ~5 N left ~19 N right ~84 N right} #### Subtract opposite-direction forces.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q18::If net external force on a system is zero, its center of mass {=has no acceleration ~must be at rest ~must move in a circle ~has zero mass} #### Zero net external force means zero center-of-mass acceleration.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q19::Torque magnitude about a pivot is {=force times perpendicular lever arm ~mass times acceleration ~pressure times volume ~force divided by time} #### Torque is tau=r_perpendicular F.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q20::Rotational equilibrium requires {=net torque equals zero ~every torque is clockwise ~no forces act ~the center of mass is outside the object} #### Clockwise and counterclockwise torques balance.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q21::A 10 N force acts perpendicular 0.50 m from a pivot. Torque magnitude is {=5 N*m ~20 N*m ~10 N*m ~0.05 N*m} #### tau=rF=0.50(10)=5 N*m.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q22::Moving a force farther from a pivot while keeping it perpendicular and unchanged makes torque {=larger ~smaller ~zero ~unchanged} #### Torque increases with lever arm.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q23::Atmospheric pressure on a sealed bottle acts {=on its outside surfaces in all directions ~only upward ~only at the cap ~only when the bottle moves} #### Fluid pressure acts normal to surfaces in all directions.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q24::A sealed flexible bottle collapses when {=outside pressure exceeds inside pressure enough to create an inward net force ~inside and outside pressures are equal ~all forces vanish ~its mass becomes zero} #### A pressure imbalance creates a net inward force.
::PHY-U1-L6-Q25::Changing the system boundary can change {=whether an interaction is classified as internal or external ~the physical interaction itself ~Newton's laws ~the SI unit of force} #### Classification depends on what the analyst places inside the boundary.

